---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import PageCTA from '../../components/PageCTA.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About Dr Sarah Cook and the Curious Brain Toolkit" description="Meet the Clinical Psychologist behind Curious Brain and discover the thinking behind the printable resources." wide>
  <PageIntro eyebrow="About Sarah & Curious Brain" title="Understanding comes first." lead="Created by Dr Sarah Cook, Clinical Psychologist, for children and the parents and professionals who support them." />
  <section class="section"><div class="container story-grid">
    <div class="creator-profile"><img src="/images/curious-brain-mascot.webp" alt="The Curious Brain mascot, a friendly brain with a magnifying glass" width="280" height="272" /><p class="eyebrow">The mind behind the toolkit</p><h2>Dr Sarah Cook</h2><p>Clinical Psychologist<br />Creator of The Curious Brain Toolkit</p><span class="profile-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span><p class="profile-caption">Psychology-informed.<br />Curiosity-led.</p></div>
    <div class="story-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Why Curious Brain?</p><h2>Making room for understanding.</h2><p>The Curious Brain Toolkit brings psychological ideas into practical conversations that children and the adults around them can explore together.</p><p>It starts with curiosity: making space to notice thoughts and feelings, understand a child’s experience and explore what might help. The child is not treated as the problem, and one strategy is not expected to work for everyone.</p><p>The printable resources offer a starting point for adults supporting children aged 8–11 at home, in school and in professional settings. They can help structure a conversation while leaving room for the child’s own words, interests and pace.</p><a class="text-link" href="/our-approach/">How the approach works <Icon name="arrow" /></a></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="values-section"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">What guides the toolkit</p><h2>Small steps. Shared understanding.</h2></div><div class="info-grid">
    <article class="info-card"><Icon name="eye" /><h3>Notice before solving</h3><p>Start with what the child is experiencing. Understanding can come before deciding what to change.</p></article>
    <article class="info-card"><Icon name="people" /><h3>Explore together</h3><p>Use the resources alongside a child, making room for conversation and their own perspective.</p></article>
    <article class="info-card"><Icon name="leaf" /><h3>Keep it manageable</h3><p>Choose a relevant starting point, try a small next step and adjust as you learn together.</p></article>
  </div></div></section>
  <PageCTA />
</BaseLayout>
